{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices across most horizons (often ~+1 to +3 EUR/t on average). Apply an upward level shift to the whole path.",
    "Underestimates rallies / trend strength: when the last ~1–2 weeks show an upswing, the model mean-reverts too quickly and misses continuation. Add a positive trend-following adjustment proportional to recent slope (e.g., 10–20 day momentum).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: the negative bias tends to grow with forecast horizon (later days are more underpredicted than early days). Use a horizon-increasing uplift (small at days 1–5, larger by days 20–30).",
    "Post-spike behavior: after sharp upward moves, the model often predicts a pullback that does not materialize (overshoots the mean-reversion). Damp mean-reversion strength after upside breaks; keep forecasts elevated for longer.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast paths are too smooth vs outcomes (miss amplitude of swings). Increase forecast variance/step size around high-momentum regimes rather than flattening toward a mid-range."
  ]
}
